        {
          "subsection_id": "intestinal_scissors_subsection_2",
          "subsection_title": "1. Introduction",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "intestinal_scissors_group_2",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Intestinal scissors are surgical scissors used to cut, divide, or dissect tissues during gastrointestinal surgery. Depending on the specific instrument, they may be used to divide bowel tissue, dissect delicate tissue planes, cut sutures, or perform other tasks during intestinal operations."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The term intestinal scissors may refer to a purpose-specific bowel scissors or to general surgical scissors selected for gastrointestinal surgery. The exact identification depends on the blade shape, length, tips, and intended use."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "OSCE identification statement:"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "“This is a pair of intestinal scissors, a surgical cutting instrument used during gastrointestinal procedures to divide tissue or perform controlled dissection. Its specific application depends on the blade design and whether the tips are sharp, blunt, straight, or curved.”",
                  "style": "quote"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "intestinal_scissors_subsection_3",
          "subsection_title": "2. Labelling and identification",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "intestinal_scissors_group_3",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/intestinal_scissors_03.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Label",
                    "Component",
                    "Function"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "A",
                      "Finger rings",
                      "Provide a grip for the operator"
                    ],
                    [
                      "B",
                      "Shanks",
                      "Connect the rings to the blades"
                    ],
                    [
                      "C",
                      "Box lock or joint",
                      "Allows the blades to pivot"
                    ],
                    [
                      "D",
                      "Blades",
                      "Cut or dissect tissue"
                    ],
                    [
                      "E",
                      "Cutting edges",
                      "Divide tissue when the blades close"
                    ],
                    [
                      "F",
                      "Tips",
                      "Guide the instrument into the intended tissue plane"
                    ],
                    [
                      "G",
                      "Ratchet",
                      "Usually absent; ordinary surgical scissors do not typically lock"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "OSCE tip: If the instrument has blunt tips, label them as blunt tips. If it has sharp tips, identify them accordingly. Do not assume every pair of scissors used in bowel surgery is a dedicated intestinal scissors."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "intestinal_scissors_subsection_4",
          "subsection_title": "3. Properties and construction",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "intestinal_scissors_group_4",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Material: Commonly surgical-grade stainless steel.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Handles: Usually finger rings.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Joint: A pivoting joint allows the blades to open and close.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Blades: May be straight or curved.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Tips: May be sharp-sharp, blunt-blunt, or sharp-blunt, depending on the model.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Blade length: Varies according to the depth and type of dissection.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Cutting action: Usually operated manually by closing the handles.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Reprocessing: Reusable models require appropriate cleaning, inspection, and sterilization.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The blades should meet correctly and cut smoothly without excessive force or lateral movement."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "intestinal_scissors_subsection_5",
          "subsection_title": "4. Classification and types",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "intestinal_scissors_group_5",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/intestinal_scissors_04.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "1. Metzenbaum scissors"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Delicate scissors with relatively long handles and shorter blades. Used for fine dissection of soft tissue, including during gastrointestinal operations."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/intestinal_scissors_05.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "2. Mayo scissors"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "More robust scissors used to cut tougher tissue and materials. Straight Mayo scissors are commonly used for sutures or materials; curved models may be used for thicker tissues, depending on the procedure."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/intestinal_scissors_06.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "3. Straight dissecting scissors"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Used for relatively direct cutting or dissection where a straight approach is appropriate."
                },
                {
                  "type": "image",
                  "assetPath": "assets/resources/content/surgical/intestinal_scissors_07.webp",
                  "caption": null
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "4. Curved dissecting scissors"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Facilitate access around structures and along curved tissue planes."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Important: Metzenbaum and Mayo scissors are general surgical instruments, not necessarily dedicated intestinal scissors. In an OSCE, identify the named instrument by its actual design rather than its use in a particular operation."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "intestinal_scissors_subsection_6",
          "subsection_title": "5. Principle of operation",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "intestinal_scissors_group_6",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Intestinal scissors work through a cutting action produced by two opposing blades."
                },
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "The blades are positioned around or within the intended tissue plane.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The operator closes the handles.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The blades rotate around the joint.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The cutting edges move past each other and divide the tissue.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The scissors are reopened and repositioned as required.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Key principle: Controlled blade movement allows tissue division while minimizing injury to adjacent structures."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "intestinal_scissors_subsection_7",
          "subsection_title": "6. Uses and indications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "intestinal_scissors_group_7",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "title": "Depending on the specific scissors, they may be used to:",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Divide selected tissues during bowel surgery.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Perform fine dissection around the intestine.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Cut sutures or ligatures when appropriate.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Divide adhesions in selected circumstances.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Assist in preparing tissues for an intestinal anastomosis.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Dissect soft tissue around vessels or other structures during gastrointestinal procedures.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "intestinal_scissors_group_8",
              "group_title": "Surgeries in which they are used",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Procedure",
                    "Purpose"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Small bowel resection",
                      "Tissue dissection and selected cutting tasks"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Colectomy",
                      "Dissection and division of appropriate soft tissues"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Intestinal anastomosis",
                      "Tissue preparation and selected dissection"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Adhesiolysis",
                      "Division of suitable adhesions using an appropriate instrument"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Gastrointestinal reconstruction",
                      "Soft-tissue dissection and other cutting tasks"
                    ]
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The actual cutting of bowel tissue may also be performed with a scalpel, electrosurgical device, stapling device, or other instrument according to the operation."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "intestinal_scissors_subsection_8",
          "subsection_title": "7. Advantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "intestinal_scissors_group_9",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Controlled cutting: Allows precise division of tissue.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Versatility: Different blade designs accommodate different tasks.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Fine dissection: Delicate scissors can separate tissue planes.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Access: Curved blades can improve access around selected structures.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Reusable designs: Many models are suitable for repeated use following appropriate reprocessing.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Manual control: The operator can adjust the extent of cutting through hand movement.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "intestinal_scissors_subsection_9",
          "subsection_title": "8. Disadvantages",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "intestinal_scissors_group_10",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Blunt or damaged blades may crush tissue rather than cut cleanly.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Excessive force can damage the joint or misalign the blades.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Inappropriate scissors may injure delicate bowel tissue.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "The blades may obscure the precise cutting plane in deep operative fields.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Using delicate scissors to cut sutures, wires, or other materials beyond their intended capacity may damage them.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Scissors are not suitable for every type of tissue division.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "intestinal_scissors_subsection_10",
          "subsection_title": "9. Complications and risks",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "intestinal_scissors_group_11",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Complication",
                    "Explanation"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Bowel injury",
                      "Incorrect blade placement may damage the bowel wall"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Bleeding",
                      "Cutting may injure blood vessels"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Injury to adjacent structures",
                      "Nearby vessels, nerves, or organs may be damaged"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Unintended tissue division",
                      "Poor visualization or inappropriate technique may lead to excessive cutting"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Tissue crushing",
                      "Blunt or misaligned blades may damage tissue"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Instrument failure",
                      "Damaged joints or blades may compromise control"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Infection",
                      "Possible if sterile technique and reprocessing are inadequate"
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "group_id": "intestinal_scissors_group_12",
              "group_title": "Prevention",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Select scissors appropriate for the tissue and task.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Inspect the blades and joint before use.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Maintain visualization of the cutting plane.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid excessive force.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Protect adjacent bowel and neurovascular structures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Do not use delicate dissecting scissors to cut materials they are not designed to cut.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "intestinal_scissors_subsection_11",
          "subsection_title": "10. Intestinal scissors versus other instruments",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "intestinal_scissors_group_13",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "table",
                  "columns": [
                    "Instrument",
                    "Main function",
                    "Key distinction"
                  ],
                  "rows": [
                    [
                      "Intestinal or dissecting scissors",
                      "Cut or dissect tissue",
                      "Two opposing cutting blades"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Doyen intestinal clamp",
                      "Temporarily occlude bowel",
                      "Long jaws designed to minimize crushing"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Bone rongeur",
                      "Remove small pieces of bone",
                      "Biting jaws"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Scalpel",
                      "Incise tissue",
                      "Single cutting blade"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Mayo scissors",
                      "Cut tougher tissue or materials, depending on design",
                      "More robust blades"
                    ],
                    [
                      "Metzenbaum scissors",
                      "Fine soft-tissue dissection",
                      "Relatively delicate blades and long shanks"
                    ]
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "intestinal_scissors_subsection_12",
          "subsection_title": "11. Precautions and contraindications",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "intestinal_scissors_group_14",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Use the correct scissors for the tissue and intended action.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid blind cutting near bowel, vessels, or other vulnerable structures.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Use fine dissecting scissors for delicate dissection when appropriate.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Do not use delicate scissors to cut thick sutures, wires, or other resistant materials unless designed for that purpose.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Avoid using scissors with damaged blades or a loose joint.",
                      "level": 0
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Maintain appropriate instrument handling and sterile technique.",
                      "level": 0
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "intestinal_scissors_subsection_13",
          "subsection_title": "12. Care, cleaning and sterilization",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "intestinal_scissors_group_15",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "list",
                  "items": [
                    {
                      "text": "Remove gross contamination promptly.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "1."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Open the blades fully.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "2."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Clean the blades, joint, shanks, and finger rings thoroughly.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "3."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Rinse and dry according to the manufacturer's instructions.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "4."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Inspect the cutting edges for chips, corrosion, or dullness.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "5."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Check that the blades meet correctly and open and close smoothly.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "6."
                    },
                    {
                      "text": "Sterilize using a validated method compatible with the instrument.",
                      "level": 0,
                      "label": "7."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Scissors that are blunt, misaligned, or damaged should be assessed and repaired or replaced before further use."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "subsection_id": "intestinal_scissors_subsection_14",
          "subsection_title": "13. High-yield OSCE viva questions",
          "subsection_icon": null,
          "content_groups": [
            {
              "group_id": "intestinal_scissors_group_16",
              "group_title": "",
              "group_subtitle": null,
              "group_icon": null,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q1. What are intestinal scissors?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Surgical scissors used for cutting or dissecting tissues during gastrointestinal procedures; the exact function depends on the instrument design."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q2. What is their principle of operation?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Two opposing blades rotate around a joint and cut tissue as they close."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q3. Mention two types of scissors used in gastrointestinal surgery."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Metzenbaum scissors and Mayo scissors are common examples of general surgical scissors that may be used during gastrointestinal procedures."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q4. What is the difference between Metzenbaum and Mayo scissors?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Metzenbaum scissors are generally more delicate and used for fine soft-tissue dissection, whereas Mayo scissors have more robust blades and are used for tougher tissues or materials appropriate to their design."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q5. Why are curved scissors useful?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "They can facilitate access and dissection around structures or along curved tissue planes."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q6. Mention four complications."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Bowel injury, bleeding, injury to adjacent structures, and tissue crushing from damaged or blunt blades."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q7. Why should scissors be inspected before use?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "To ensure the blades are sharp, properly aligned, undamaged, and able to open and close smoothly."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q8. How do scissors differ from a scalpel?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Scissors use two opposing blades, whereas a scalpel uses a single cutting blade."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q9. Can all scissors be used to cut sutures?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "No. The scissors must be suitable for the material; using delicate dissecting scissors on resistant materials may damage their cutting edges."
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Q10. What is the key safety principle?"
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Use the correct scissors, maintain visualization, and control blade movement to avoid injury to the bowel and adjacent structures."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
